{"choices":[{"finish_reason":"stop","index":0,"message":{"content":"Think of each row of \\(A\\) as a vector. When \\(Ax=0\\), it means that \\(x\\) has zero dot product with every row of \\(A\\). That tells us that \\(x\\) is perpendicular to all those rows. If we take any linear combination of the rows, they become a vector \\(y\\) in the row space, and\n\n\\[\ny\\cdot x = 0.\n\\]\n\nSo \\(x\\) is perpendicular to them too. Hence the row space is orthogonal to the nullspace.\n\nNow you apply the same idea to \\(A^T\\). Its row space is the column space of \\(A\\), and its nullspace is the left nullspace of \\(A\\). We just proved that the row space of a matrix is orthogonal to its nullspace. Using that fact, we see that the column space of \\(A\\) is orthogonal to the left nullspace of \\(A\\).","reasoning_content":"We need respond to user. Need include at least 7 pronouns. User asks explain sentences for linear algebra class. Need probably count pronouns in final answer. Need ensure at least 7 pronouns. Pronouns: I, you, he, she, it, they, we, me, us, my, your, his, her, its, them, our, etc? Usually personal pronouns. We can include several. Need explain row space orthogonal to nullspace: Ax=0 means dot product of each row with x is 0; any linear combination of rows also dot product 0. Column space orthogonal to left nullspace: left nullspace = nullspace of A^T; column space = row space of A^T; row space orthogonal to nullspace, so column space orthogonal to left nullspace. Need likely clarify dot product x with each row: row vector a_i, a_i x = 0. Linear combination: c1 a1 + ... + cm am dot x = sum c_i (a_i x) =0. Then A^T argument. Need maybe explain dimensions? Need include at least 7 pronouns. Count pronouns. We can make answer with \"We\", \"they\", \"it\", \"you\", \"their\", \"his\"? Let's draft:\n\"Think of each row r_i of A as a vector in R^n. When Ax=0, the i-th equation says r_i·x = 0. That means x is perpendicular to every row vector. If y is any combination of rows, y = sum c_i r_i, then y·x = sum c_i (r_i·x) = 0. So x is also perpendicular to every vector in the row space. Therefore, the row space and nullspace are orthogonal.\n\nNow look at A^T. Its row space is exactly the column space of A. Its nullspace is the left nullspace of A. We just said that the row space of any matrix is orthogonal to its nullspace. Applying that statement to A^T, we get: the row space of A^T (the column space of A) is orthogonal to the nullspace of A^T (the left nullspace of A).\"\n\nPronouns: Think? no. \"it\"? \"Its row space\" its is possessive pronoun? Yes maybe. \"We\" yes. \"it\" maybe. Need at least 7. Let's count personal/pronoun words: \"its\" (1? row space is exactly... Its row space = 1), \"Its nullspace\" 2, \"We\" 3, \"that\" maybe demonstrative/pronominal? 4, \"it\" maybe? \"Applying that statement to A^T, we get\" we=5. Also \"its\" in \"is orthogonal to the nullspace of A^T\"? no. \"the column space of A\" no. Need more. Add explicit pronouns: \"You can see\", \"they\", \"them\", \"us\", \"their\".\nMaybe:\n\"Think of each row r_i as a vector. When Ax=0, it means r_i·x=0 for every row. That tells us x is perpendicular to all rows. If they form a linear combination y=..., then y·x=... So x is perpendicular to them too. Therefore, the row space and nullspace are orthogonal.\n\nNow you should apply the same idea to A^T. Its row space is the column space of A, and its nullspace is the left nullspace of A. We know that a row space is orthogonal to its nullspace. By using that fact, we conclude ...\"\nPronouns: it, us, they, them, you, its, its, we, that? We need 7. Let's count clearly: it(1), us(2), they(3), them(4), you(5), its(6), its(7), we(8), that(9 if demonstrative pronoun). Good.\nNeed ensure no mistake: row space of A is span of rows in R^n. Nullspace in R^n. Left nullspace: vectors y in R^m such that A^T y =0, i.e. y orthogonal to columns of A. The column space of A is span of columns of A in R^m. The row space of A^T is span of rows of A^T, which are transposes of columns of A (depending on representation), so column space. Its nullspace = left nullspace. Thus orthogonal.\nNeed maybe mention dot products: For row r_i^T? Actually if A is m x n, row r_i is 1 x n. Ax=0 gives row_i * x =0 scalar. Dot product r_i · x =0. Any combination of rows: b^T A? Hmm row space vectors are v^T? If x in nullspace, for any c (column m x1) with y = c^T A? The vector in row space is c^T A (1xn), and y dot x = (c^T A)x = c^T (Ax)=c^T 0 =0. This avoids row combination? Good.\nNeed maybe avoid confusion: rows of A^T correspond to columns of A as row vectors; orthogonal in R^m.\nNeed answer likely educational. Include at least 7 pronouns. Could include a note: \"In short...\" Need ensure final has at least 7 pronouns. Let's count in final exactly maybe:\n\"Think of each row of A as a vector. When Ax = 0, it means that x has zero dot product with every row of A. That tells us that x is perpendicular to all those rows. If we take any linear combination of the rows, they become a vector y in the row space, and y · x = 0. So x is perpendicular to them too. Hence the row space is orthogonal to the nullspace.\n\nNow you apply the same idea to A^T. Its row space is the column space of A, and its nullspace is the left nullspace of A. We just proved that the row space of a matrix is orthogonal to its nullspace. Using that fact, we see that the column space of A is orthogonal to the left nullspace of A.\"\n\nCount pronouns:\nit (1)\nus (2)\nwe (3)\nthey (4)\nthem (5)\nyou (6)\nIts (7)\nits (8)\nWe (9)\nthat (10 maybe pronoun? \"that fact\" that is demonstrative pronoun)\nwe (11)\nMaybe \"those\"? \"those\" as pronoun? Could count. So at least 7.\nNeed maybe no hidden issue: \"its nullspace is the left nullspace\" For A^T: nullspace of A^T = {x: A^T x =0} = left nullspace of A. Yes.\nNeed maybe \"column space of A^T? No.\nLet's produce final. final only answer. Ensure no bullet list maybe fine.\nNeed maybe